.TH RMUCTL 1 "2026-09-04" "rmu 0.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rmuctl \- inspect, restore, and maintain the FreeDesktop Trash
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rmuctl
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTION ]
.B list
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B rmuctl
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTION ]
.B info
.I ID...
.br
.B rmuctl
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTION ]
.B restore
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.I ID...
.br
.B rmuctl
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTION ]
.B purge
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.I ID...
.br
.B rmuctl
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTION ]
.B empty
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B rmuctl
.RI [ GLOBAL-OPTION ]
.B doctor
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B rmuctl
.B completions
.I SHELL COMMAND
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rmuctl
is the stateless companion to
.BR rmu (1).
Trash-management commands discover and validate the standard home and
mounted-filesystem Trash directories on every invocation. RMU has no daemon,
database, private Trash location, or automatic-emptying policy.
.PP
Mutating commands identify entries by the stable IDs emitted by
.BR list .
An ID is exactly 32 lowercase hexadecimal characters.
Each entry's root, payload, metadata, and incarnation are revalidated
immediately before a change.  A previously printed ID is never treated as
permanent authority.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options may appear before or after Trash-management commands. Output
format and progress options are not accepted by
.BR completions ;
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
are process-wide selectors recognized anywhere.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON or JSON Lines.  Path and name fields include authoritative lowercase
hexadecimal byte companions for arbitrary Unix names.
.TP
.B \-\-null
Terminate
.B list
records with NUL.  This option is valid only with
.BR list .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress successful action output.  Warnings and errors remain visible.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable color.  Accepted for stable scripting; output is currently plain.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS list
List known entries with stable ID, deletion date, allocated size when known,
health state, and original path.  Healthy rows are still emitted if another
Trash root cannot be inspected, but the command warns and exits nonzero so a
partial inventory cannot be mistaken for a complete one.
.TP
.BI \-\-filesystem " PATH"
Select entries associated with PATH's filesystem.
.TP
.BI \-\-older-than " DURATION"
Select entries older than DURATION.
.TP
.BI \-\-newer-than " DURATION"
Select entries newer than DURATION.
.TP
.BI \-\-sort " FIELD"
Sort by
.BR name ,
.BR date ,
.BR size ,
or
.BR path .
The default is date.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the selected ordering.
.SS info
Show the original path, desktop metadata, payload location, allocated size,
and structural problems for each selected ID.  At least one ID is required.
.SS restore
Restore healthy entries without overwriting an existing destination.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " DIRECTORY"
Restore below DIRECTORY instead of the recorded original path.
.TP
.BI \-\-conflict " POLICY"
Use
.B fail
(the default), or choose a new
.I .restored.N
name with
.BR rename .
.TP
.B \-\-parents
Create missing destination parents.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Report the planned destination without changing anything.
.PP
A cross-filesystem restore fails rather than silently copying.
.SS purge
Permanently remove the selected IDs.  At least one ID is required.  This
command prompts unless standard input is not a terminal, in which case an
explicit
.B \-\-yes
is required.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Report matching entries without prompting or changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Confirm permanent deletion noninteractively.
.PP
A directory payload containing a mounted filesystem boundary is refused.
Mount identity is checked again during traversal so a newly introduced bind or
filesystem mount is never traversed for deletion.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress [= WHEN ]
Show the number of processed and selected entries.  WHEN is
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
.SS empty
Permanently remove all selected entries, subject to optional filters.  It uses
the same confirmation and progress options as
.BR purge .
.TP
.BI \-\-filesystem " PATH"
Restrict the operation to the Trash associated with PATH's filesystem.
.TP
.BI \-\-older-than " DURATION"
Remove only entries older than DURATION.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
List what would be removed without prompting or changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Confirm permanent deletion noninteractively.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress [= WHEN ]
Show the number of processed and selected entries.
.SS doctor
Diagnose missing payload/metadata pairs, malformed metadata, unsafe roots,
interrupted-operation quarantine files, and broken directory-size caches.
With no repair option, doctor is read-only.
Repair and recovery options are refused when Trash-root discovery is
incomplete, and an incompletely enumerated root is never mutated.  A broken
.I directorysizes
cache is advisory: it produces a warning but does not make the authoritative
payload and metadata inventory incomplete.  An explicit repair is permitted
only when those namespaces were completely enumerated, and no replacement is
published if any selected directory changes or cannot be counted completely.
.TP
.B \-\-repair-cache
Atomically rebuild FreeDesktop
.I directorysizes
caches.  This does not delete orphaned data.
.TP
.BI \-\-recover-orphans " DIRECTORY"
Move recoverable orphan data into DIRECTORY without overwriting.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Report repairs without changing anything.
.SS completions
Print exactly one shell completion definition to standard output without
discovering or reading Trash.
.I SHELL
must be
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
or
.BR fish .
.I COMMAND
must be
.B rmu
or
.BR rmuctl .
Packages install these definitions in each shell's standard system vendor
directory; this command supports custom per-user completion layouts.
.SH DURATION FORMAT
Durations are a nonnegative integer followed by one suffix:
.B w
(weeks),
.B d
(days),
.B h
(hours),
.B m
(minutes), or
.B s
(seconds).  Examples are
.BR 30d ,
.BR 12h ,
and
.BR 90m .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RMU_PROGRESS
Default progress policy for
.B purge
and
.BR empty :
.BR never ,
.BR auto ,
or
.BR always .
It is ignored by read-only commands and overridden by an explicit progress
option.
.PP
No
.B rmuctl
environment variable can imply
.BR \-\-yes ,
permanent deletion, repair, recovery, conflict renaming, parent creation, or
overwrite.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested action succeeded and, when applicable, the Trash inventory was
complete.  A successful doctor repair returns zero only when its post-action
inventory has no unresolved problem.
.TP
.B 1
Discovery or inventory was partial, confirmation was not given, a selected ID
was ambiguous or stale for the requested action, an action failed, or the
command line or environment setting was invalid. Merely listing or inspecting
a known unhealthy entry does not itself change the exit status.  Doctor returns
one when a problem remains unresolved or a repair action fails; an initial
problem that was successfully repaired does not force a failure status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B rmuctl list --sort=size --reverse
List the largest known Trash entries first.
.TP
.B rmuctl --json info ENTRY-ID
Emit complete machine-readable information for one entry.
.TP
.B rmuctl restore --conflict=rename ENTRY-ID
Restore without overwriting, choosing a new destination name if necessary.
.TP
.B rmuctl empty --older-than=30d --dry-run
Preview removing Trash entries older than 30 days.
.TP
.B rmuctl empty --older-than=30d --yes --progress
Perform that operation with progress and no prompt.
.TP
.B rmuctl completions zsh rmu > ~/.zfunc/_rmu
Write the
.B rmu
definition into a custom Zsh completion directory.
.TP
.B rmuctl completions zsh rmuctl > ~/.zfunc/_rmuctl
Write the companion command's definition into the same directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rmu (1),
.BR rm (1)
.UR https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash/latest/
FreeDesktop Trash Specification 1.0
.UE
